-**Rule:** If the damage description explicitly mentions the word "blend" for any part, that part must be added to **"blendableAdjacentCategories"**, even if it doesn't meet other adjacency conditions or the **BLENDING RULES**

BLENDING RULES:
- Only **outer metal body panels** are considered blendable (exception hood, liftgates & trunks are never a blendable part).
- Blendable panels: doors, fenders, quarter panels, trunk lid,rocker panels,vehicle pillars (A/B/C)
- If main outer part of a category is already in damaged lines, then it cannot be a blendable category or any other category.
- Non-blendable parts: Front Bumper / Rear Bumper, glass/windows, lamps/lights, grilles, roof and small trims
---------
REPAIR-BASED ADJACENT BLENDING RULE (ENHANCED):

- If a panel has a "Repair" operation, include its adjacent blendable categories in "blendableAdjacentCategories" ONLY when at least one of the following conditions is met:

  1. SEAM PROXIMITY CONDITION:
     - The repair area is within approximately **2–3 cm** of the shared seam with the adjacent panel, OR
     - The damage, deformation, scratches, or repair preparation visibly reaches the seam line or panel edge.

  2. LARGE-AREA CONDITION:
     - The repair extends across a **large portion of the panel**, such that refinishing would reasonably require paint blending into an adjacent panel to achieve color match.
     - "Large portion" excludes small, isolated dents, chips, or scratches that are localized and confined to a limited area.

  3. EXPLICIT BLEND OVERRIDE:
     - The damage description explicitly mentions blending an adjacent panel (e.g., "blend quarter panel", "blend into door").
     - This condition overrides seam distance and area size checks.

- Adjacent panels MUST NOT be included for blending when:
  - The repair area is **localized, minor, or centered** on the panel and is clearly **more than 3 cm away from any seam**, AND
  - There is no explicit blend instruction in the damage description.

- If seam proximity or repair extent **cannot be confidently determined** from the image or damage description, default to **NOT adding** any adjacent blendable categories.







---------
- If the repair area is **localized, minor, or positioned far from the seam** (e.g., small scratches or dents on the opposite edge), **do not include** adjacent panels for blending.
- If panel has a "Remove/Replace" operation its adjacent blendable categories should be in ***"blendableAdjacentCategories"***
----
TRUCK-SIDE CAB PREFERENCE (This Rule only applies to Pickup trucks only)
- If the vehicle is a pickup truck and a damaged line is a side/bed/quarter panel (bedside, quarter panel, side body), then:
  1. If the main CAB (the upper cab body panel) is present in AVAILABLE_CATEGORIES and is NOT listed as damaged with confidence >= MIN_CONFIDENCE_FOR_PRESENCE, the CAB should be selected as the PRIMARY blend target.
